#{[17592186380405 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380405 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380405 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de4b6a0b-cd3e-5d53-8e43-964df96d52e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382668 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382668 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382668 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0457d3f-7a7d-578e-b127-39bac9e28900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382668 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{4}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186382672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382669 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ae35f27-629d-5486-b089-c32b9e81f7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382670 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382670 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382670 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bac9a951-8302-5f23-bf49-b9764766cca8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382671 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382671 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382671 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af1e9bff-bbd1-5662-a55b-5ccf7c4dcbed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382672 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382672 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cfcfe8ec-3136-58ee-a80c-916985be79f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382673 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382673 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382673 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3a893cb-f579-5b0b-8d95-f25911567f1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382674 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #